        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Hey,\n\nThanks for getting back to be so quickly.\n\nI'd just like to explain what mini.ai does for me, and why I use this, and why this wouldn't be ideal for me.\n\nI'm not interested in changing how I interact with vim motions by adding descriptors such as `an` / `in` and more. For me, that changes the \"vim-language\". Adding more targets/text-objects is fine, and preferred.\n\nFor me, mini.ai adds more great text-objects, such as brackets, argument, comma, and more.\n\nAdditionally, the `cover_or_next` improves upon the default vim behavior in regards to text-objects with identical open/closing character such as strings. For instance, when standing in between two strings, vim doesn't correctly understand that my di\" operation want to delete the contents of the following string, and instead deletes my content in between the two strings on the same line where my cursor currently is. It's purist, but doesn't do what I want.\n\n| = cursor position\n\"first string\" content in |between string \"second string\"\n\nI would like di\" to delete the contents of the \"second string\" here, but native vim deletes the content in between the strings.\n\n`cover_or_next` fixes the above case, however, instead it breaks the multi-line cover of native vim motions.\n\nI tried a small change that fixes both cases and in my opinion results in an improvement over the regular algorithm in vim without changing any muscle memory.\n\nI just quickly threw something together to try this out, here's the [commit on my fork](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/compare/main...LarssonMartin1998:mini.nvim:feat/cover-across-lines). Not saying this is the best solution, but it solves the problem I'm having.",
